# tools/crypto_kumara_subkey_witness.rish — the Kumara subkey, Season G's NINETEENTH composition: a per-record subkey, the hardened child
# m/44'/coin'/account'/index'/record' of the identity node, so a keeper signs each record with a DISTINCT key while every subkey descends
# deterministically from the one Kumara identity (Season G · Cryptography).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography · Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
# Design read: active-designing/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md names "a per-record subkey carry (a child of
# the identity node)" as the identity arc's next rung; the 1,024-round itinerary, Season 2 Journey 6 (Provenance), wants one identity to sign
# many records without spending the same key on each.
# Kin: crypto/kumara_identity.rye (the identity this steps beneath, GREEN) · crypto/slip10_ed25519.rye (the ckd_priv this walks one level, GREEN) ·
# crypto/ed25519_sign.rye + crypto/ed25519_verify.rye (the signature, GREEN) · crypto/signed_carry.rye + crypto/kumara_carry.rye (the frame this
# seals and the binding it reuses, GREEN). Monocypher (vendor/monocypher, CC0/BSD-dual) is the parity TARGET of the primitives beneath, never a
# copied line — our own composition, studied clean-room (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# crypto/kumara_subkey.rye authors NO new cryptography and adds NO new frame — the step from identity node to hardened child is
# crypto/slip10_ed25519.rye's own ckd_priv, and sealing AS a subkey writes a Grain Signed Carry v1 frame reusing crypto/kumara_carry.rye's
# binding. Purely local over TEST identities from published vectors — no real identity key, no network, no funds, no real device; deriving a real
# keeper's subkeys and signing a real record with one stays the custody gate, and constant-time timing-safety stays the named horizon. ed25519
# admits only hardened derivation, so a subkey's public key cannot be derived from the parent's public alone — the parent→subkey attestation (the
# identity signing that a subkey is its own) is the named next rung; this rung proves the derivation and per-record separation beneath it.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/crypto_kumara_subkey_witness.rish

say "crypto-kumara-subkey: Language EN — Season G's NINETEENTH composition: a per-record subkey, the hardened child m/44'/coin'/account'/index'/record' of the identity node. Where crypto/kumara_identity.rye derives the ONE identity a keeper signs with, this rung derives a DISTINCT signing key per record beneath it, so a keeper does not reuse a single signing key across every record they seal, while every subkey still descends deterministically from the one identity — the reason crypto/kumara_identity.rye carries a chain code at its node."
say "crypto-kumara-subkey: Style Radiant — one identity, a fresh key for each record it keeps. The step from parent node to hardened child is slip10's own ckd_priv, already proven against SLIP-0010's ed25519 vectors; this rung reads the identity's chain code and steps one level deeper, then reads off the subkey's public key the same two independent ways the identity door does. Sealing AS a subkey adds no frame — it writes a signed carry under the subkey's seed and reuses the Kumara carry binding, so a record opens as exactly the subkey it was sealed with and no other."
say "crypto-kumara-subkey: Lens TAME — the composition authors no new cryptography; it wires a proven ckd_priv step to a proven carry, bounds the record level below 2³¹, and asserts its contracts (the identity's node-bearing fields are the widths a slip10 node holds; the child sits one depth below the identity; the subkey's two public derivations agree; the sealed frame's signer equals the subkey's public key). GREEN on metal three replayable ways: anchored to the identity arc (the subkey at record r equals walking ckd_priv from the same identity node by hand, its public key equal to both slip10's public form tag-stripped and ed25519_sign's independent derive_public); separation (same identity + record → identical subkey, different record → different subkey, every subkey differs from its parent); and a REAL working signing key (it signs a message verify accepts, a flipped message byte and a flipped signature byte refuse, and it seals a record open_as verifies as the subkey while refusing the parent identity and a sibling subkey with UnexpectedSigner across six payload lengths, a record at the hardened offset refusing NotHardened). Purely local — TEST identities only, no real identity key, no network, no funds, no device; deriving a real keeper's subkeys stays the custody gate."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "crypto/bin/kumara_subkey"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build crypto/kumara_subkey.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "crypto-kumara-subkey: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "crypto/bin/kumara_subkey.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "crypto-kumara-subkey: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "crypto-kumara-subkey: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN crypto-kumara-subkey' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "crypto-kumara-subkey: selftest did not print the GREEN crypto-kumara-subkey line"
